## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of 128-bit bit packing, non-overlapping fields, and in-place updates without loops. It exercises careful mask calculation and cross-boundary writes.

## Key Concepts
- Bit masks and clearing specific ranges
- Cross-boundary placement across lo/hi
- Safe in-place updates without branching
- Endianness-agnostic bit numbering

## Code Example
```c
void packHeader128(uint64_t hi, uint64_t lo, uint8_t A, uint16_t B, uint16_t C, uint32_t D, uint64_t *out_hi, uint64_t *out_lo) {
  uint64_t lo_mask = ((uint64_t)0xFF) | (((uint64_t)0xFFF) << 8) | (((uint64_t)0xFFFF) << 32);
  uint64_t hi_mask = ((uint64_t)0xFFFFFFFF) << 16;
  *out_lo = (lo & ~lo_mask) | (uint64_t)A | (((uint64_t)B) << 8) | (((uint64_t)C) << 32);
  *out_hi = (hi & ~hi_mask) | (((uint64_t)D) << 16);
}
``` 

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt if ranges could overlap and you needed deterministic behavior?
- How would you validate correctness across all inputs in a unit test?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to perform cross-128-bit bit counting without loops, using portable bit-twiddling. Demonstrates knowledge of XOR for difference, and multi-word popcount without hardware intrinsics.

## Key Concepts

- XOR-based difference
- SWAR-style popcount on 64-bit words
- Combining counts for 128-bit data

## Code Example

```c
unsigned distance128(uint128 a, uint128 b) {
  uint64_t x = a.hi ^ b.hi;
  uint64_t y = a.lo ^ b.lo;
  // SWAR popcount on x
  x = x - ((x >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
  x = (x & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((x >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  x = (x + (x >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  x = x + (x >> 8);
  x = x + (x >> 16);
  x = x + (x >> 32);
  // SWAR popcount on y
  y = y - ((y >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
  y = (y & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((y >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  y = (y + (y >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  y = y + (y >> 8);
  y = y + (y >> 16);
  y = y + (y >> 32);
  return (unsigned)(x & 0xFF) + (unsigned)(y & 0xFF);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you generalize to arbitrary n-word widths while maintaining no-loops constraints?
- Could you optimize using compiler intrinsics if available and fall back gracefully? If so, which intrinsics and how would you keep portability?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to reason about data-parallel bit tricks and boundary-safe shifts without branches.

## Key Concepts

- Byte-wise (SWAR) operations across 128-bit lanes
- Constructing per-byte masks to constrain operations to byte boundaries
- Handling n in 0..7 without loops or conditionals

## Code Example

```cpp
// outline of approach (conceptual; leverages 128-bit value split into bytes)
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;
static inline uint128 perByteRotateLeft128(uint128 x, unsigned n){
    uint128 M = { 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFULL, 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFULL };
    uint128 left  = { .hi = (x.hi << n) | (x.lo >> (8 - n)), .lo = x.lo << n };
    uint128 right = { .hi = (x.hi >> (8 - n)), .lo = (x.lo >> (8 - n)) };
    return { .hi = (left.hi & M.hi) | (right.hi & ~M.hi),
             .lo = (left.lo & M.lo) | (right.lo & ~M.lo) };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you generalize to 32-bit lanes or to 256-bit types?
- What are the performance implications on modern compilers and ISAs?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to extend known 64-bit bit tricks to 128-bit values without branches. Emphasizes portability and optimization.

## Key Concepts
- SWAR parallel bit counting
- Masks and shifts across 64-bit lanes
- Combining results from hi/lo halves

## Code Example
```javascript
static inline unsigned popcount64(uint64_t x) {
  x = x - ((x >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
  x = (x & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((x >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  x = (x + (x >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  x = x + (x >> 8);
  x = x + (x >> 16);
  x = x + (x >> 32);
  return (unsigned)(x & 0x7F);
}
unsigned countBits128(uint128 x) {
  return popcount64(x.hi) + popcount64(x.lo);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you use hardware intrinsics if allowed?
- How would you extend to 256-bit types or streaming data?

## Why This Is Asked
Branchless 128-bit comparison is common in crypto/hash pipelines and requires careful borrow logic across halves. It tests low-level reasoning without relying on 128-bit native ops.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit numbers split into hi/lo.
- Borrow propagation across 64-bit halves.
- Branchless evaluation using comparisons on unsigned integers.

## Code Example
```c
uint8_t lessThan128(uint128 a, uint128 b){
  uint64_t loDiff = a.lo - b.lo;
  uint64_t borrowLo = (a.lo < b.lo);
  uint64_t hiDiff = a.hi - b.hi - borrowLo;
  uint64_t borrowHi = (a.hi < b.hi + borrowLo);
  return (uint8_t)borrowHi;
}
```

